  "content": {
    "id": "p31-q56-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "56",
    "prompt": "Given $a\\cos^2\\theta+b\\sin^2\\theta=c$, where $a\\ne b$, express $\\cos 2\\theta$ in terms of $a$, $b$ and $c$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\cos 2\\theta=\\dfrac{2c-a-b}{a-b}$.\n\nReplace the squared functions using the cosine double-angle identities and rearrange.",
      "worked": "Using $\\cos^2\\theta=\\dfrac{1+\\cos 2\\theta}{2}$ and $\\sin^2\\theta=\\dfrac{1-\\cos 2\\theta}{2}$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}c&=\\frac{a(1+\\cos 2\\theta)+b(1-\\cos 2\\theta)}{2}\\\\2c&=a+b+(a-b)\\cos 2\\theta\\\\(a-b)\\cos 2\\theta&=2c-a-b\\\\\\cos 2\\theta&=\\frac{2c-a-b}{a-b}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 46
  },
